    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Chef Barinov invents a complaint about a faulty kitchen oven to distract Eleonora. When Nagiyev strolls past calling Eleonora his queen, Chef drags Mikhail into his office to sleep it off.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня на кухне печь барахлит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Боюсь, нам бракованную подсунули.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Хорошо, найдёшь Мишу, скажи ему.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, пойду и обязательно скажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Чего этот тип радостный такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У него давно башка барахлит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Пусти меня к нему сейчас!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, Мишаня, силы слишком неравны.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он трезвый, а тебе надо выспаться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "У тебя здесь есть выпить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть диван, ложись.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай отдыхай, а я принесу.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня на кухне печь барахлит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The oven in my kitchen is acting up.' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'is acting up' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Боюсь, нам бракованную подсунули.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am afraid they slipped us a defective one.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'am afraid' and finishing on 'slipped'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "eleonora",
      "t": "Хорошо, найдёшь Мишу, скажи ему.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, when you find Misha, tell him.' In Russian, the idea of 'well' flows naturally into 'to him' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, пойду и обязательно скажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine, I will go and be sure to tell him.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'all right' and finishing on 'will say'. Mikhail answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Чего этот тип радостный такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why is that guy so cheerful?' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'what' and 'this' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У него давно башка барахлит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'His head has been acting up for a long time.' In Russian, the idea of 'at' flows naturally into 'is acting up' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Пусти меня к нему сейчас!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let me go to him right now!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'let go' and finishing on 'now'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ладно, Мишаня, силы слишком неравны.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, Mishanya, the odds are too uneven.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'all right', while 'uneven' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он трезвый, а тебе надо выспаться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is sober, and you need to sleep it off.' In Russian, the idea of 'he' flows naturally into 'get enough sleep' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "У тебя здесь есть выпить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you have anything to drink here?' Mikhail anchors the line on 'at', while 'to drink' completes the thought. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть диван, ложись.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have a couch, lie down.' In Russian, the idea of 'at' flows naturally into 'lie down' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай отдыхай, а я принесу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go on and rest, and I will bring it.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'will bring'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
